  "response": "I’ll verify the handler’s current signature so the migration keeps its external contract unchanged. I’ll use the repository’s risk-check guidance because this is a TypeScript hot-path investigation.\n\nThe responsible finding is `sync_io_in_hotpath` in `loadPlanSchema`.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> “`existsSync` @L12 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  \n> “`statSync` @L13 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”  \n> “`readFileSync` @L15 in `loadPlanSchema` (domain function)”\n\nAll three methods block the event loop on every request: `existsSync`, `statSync`, and `readFileSync`.\n\nThe smallest migration is to replace all three operations with one awaited `readFile` from `node:fs/promises`. It can preserve the exported name and zero-argument calling shape, while retaining the existing missing-file and empty-file behavior:\n\n```ts\nimport { readFile } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n\nexport async function loadPlanSchema(): Promise<unknown> {\n  try {\n    const contents = await readFile(SCHEMA_PATH, \"utf8\");\n    if (contents.length === 0) return null;\n    return JSON.parse(contents);\n  } catch (error) {\n    if (\n      error instanceof Error &&\n      \"code\" in error &&\n      error.code === \"ENOENT\"\n    ) {\n      return null;\n    }\n    throw error;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nCallers must `await loadPlanSchema()`. That necessarily changes the return contract from `unknown` to `Promise<unknown>`—there is no way to preserve synchronous return semantics while performing genuinely asynchronous disk I/O. The export name and parameters remain unchanged, and eliminating the separate existence/stat checks also avoids redundant filesystem calls and their check-then-read race.",
